.TH CHTTP_RUN_QUERY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_run_query \- run one request with explicit headers, with the default client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_run_query(chttp_method_t method, const char *url,
                              const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                              chmap headers,
                              chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This is a short wrapper that builds a chttp_request_t inside, attaches
headers to that request without a transfer of ownership, calls chttp_do
and then frees the request object that it built. Among the short
functions for the default client, this is the only one that takes a
header set of your choice in one call; with chttp_get, chttp_post and
the others you must instead build a chttp_request_t by hand, with a
call to chttp_request_new and then one call to
chttp_request_set_header for each header.

The caller keeps the full ownership of headers. The function only
borrows headers for the time of the call, and never frees or changes
headers.

The keys in headers can use any case, for example "Host",
"Content-Type" or "Accept". This module adds its own default value for
some keys (host, accept, user-agent, content-length, content-type,
authorization and expect), and matches each of them against headers
without regard to case. A header of yours with a natural case
therefore stops the matching default of the module, and the two do not
both go onto the wire.

A chmap matches a key by its exact bytes, so headers can hold two keys
that differ as bytes but are the same header name under the
case-insensitive rules of HTTP, such as "Host" and "host" together.
chttp_request_set_header makes every key lowercase before it stores
that key, so it can never make this situation, but a headers map that
you build directly gives no such guarantee. Only one key of such a pair
reaches the wire, and never both: the key that went into headers last
wins, which matches the behaviour of an update with one exact key. The
client drops the other key, and does not send it as a second header
line.

You give headers to this function directly, so it does not go through
the validation of chttp_request_set_header. No key and no value in it
may hold a CR byte or an LF byte inside it: this is the same rule as
in chttp_request_set_header, for the same reason, because data with a
CRLF in it can otherwise inject extra header lines or cut the request
into two. The function gives
ccol_invalid_args for such a
headers map, before it opens any connection. headers must also hold no
"Transfer-Encoding" key, in any case, for the reason that
chttp_request_set_header rejects that name: this client never applies
a transfer coding to a request body, so it cannot obey such a header,
and on the wire that header sits beside the automatic Content-Length
of this module, over a body with no transfer coding, which is an
ambiguous framing.

The function DOES accept a "Content-Length" key in headers, but it
checks that key on a request that carries a body (POST, PUT or PATCH).
A good value is a plain unsigned string of decimal digits, with no
leading '+' or '-' and no whitespace inside it, and it must also match
the real length of the body that the client sends. The function gives
ccol_invalid_args for a bad value, once the request reaches the wire.
See chttpclient_do(3) and chttp_request_set_header(3) for the same
check and for the same reason: the declared framing and the wire must
not disagree.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method     The HTTP method.
url        The target URL. It must not be NULL.
body       The request body, or NULL for a request with no body.
headers    A chmap(char* -> char*) of the request headers, or NULL for
           no headers. The function borrows it for the time of the
           call, and does not consume it or free it. No key and no
           value may hold a CR byte or an LF byte, and no key may be
           "Transfer-Encoding".
resp_out   It must not be NULL. The function sets it to NULL at once,
           and if the call succeeds, it then gets the pointer to the
           response.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The same codes as chttpclient_do. The function gives
ccol_not_enough_memory if the library cannot allocate the internal
request object, and ccol_invalid_args if url or resp_out is NULL, if
headers holds a key or a value with a CR byte or an LF byte in it, or
if headers holds a "Transfer-Encoding" key.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttp_get (3),
.BR chttp_post (3),
.BR chttp_request_new (3),
.BR chttp_request_set_header (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3)
